const pages = [
{id:'introduction', group:'GETTING STARTED', title:'Introduction', lead:'A security-focused API authentication and authorization framework for Node.js.', html:`<h2>What is KeyPointJS?</h2><p>KeyPointJS provides keypoint identity validation, scope-aware request context, policy rules, a small HTTP router, middleware, hooks, and plugins. It uses Node.js HTTP server APIs and does not require Express or Fastify.</p><p>The project focuses on API security building blocks. It does not guarantee secure deployment by itself, and some advertised protocol options remain incomplete.</p><h2>What it is for</h2><ul><li>Identify API callers with stored keypoints.</li><li>Check keypoint expiration, request protocol, and configured origins.</li><li>Evaluate scopes and registered inheritance in handlers or policies.</li><li>Compose global rules and request plugins.</li></ul><h2>What is optional</h2><p>The HTML parser is an optional package subpath. It does not fetch pages or execute scripts. WebScraper is not implemented. gRPC, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 options should not be interpreted as complete server implementations.</p><h2>Next step</h2><p>Follow <a href="docs.html?page=installation">installation</a> and the <a href="docs.html?page=getting-started">quick start</a>.</p>`},
{id:'installation',group:'GETTING STARTED',title:'Installation',lead:'Install KeyPointJS from npm and import the public package entry point.',html:`<h2>Install the core package</h2>${snippet("npm install keypointjs")}<p>The package is an ES module and supports Node.js 18 or later. Import it using the package name rather than a path into its source directory.</p>${snippet("import { KeypointJS } from 'keypointjs';\n\nconst api = new KeypointJS();")}<h2>Optional HTML parser</h2><p>Parser dependencies are optional. A normal install includes them when npm installs optional dependencies. If you installed with <code>--omit=optional</code>, install the parser packages only if you use <code>keypointjs/html-parser</code>.</p>${snippet('npm install parse5@7.3.0 css-select@5.2.2 css-what@6.2.2')}<p>The core import does not eagerly load the HTML parser. See <a href="docs.html?page=optional-dependencies">Optional Dependencies</a>.</p><h2>Verify installation</h2>${snippet("node --input-type=module -e \"import('keypointjs').then(({KeypointJS}) => console.log(typeof KeypointJS))\"")}<p>Expected output: <code>function</code>.</p>`},
{id:'requirements',group:'GETTING STARTED',title:'Requirements',lead:'Runtime and package expectations verified from the project package metadata.',html:`<h2>Runtime</h2><ul><li>Node.js <strong>18 or later</strong>.</li><li>ECMAScript modules (ESM): package metadata sets <code>"type": "module"</code>.</li><li>The HTTP security core uses built-in Node.js modules.</li></ul><h2>Dependencies</h2><p><code>ws</code> is a required dependency. HTML parsing uses optional <code>parse5</code>, <code>css-select</code>, and <code>css-what</code> dependencies. Optional native WebSocket accelerators may also be installed by npm.</p><h2>Package version</h2><p>The website package reference was checked against the linked repository package metadata: <strong>1.3.0</strong>. The npm registry may change independently from this website commit; check the npm package page for the currently published version.</p><h2>Environment</h2><p>The package currently targets Node.js and does not provide browser runtime support for its framework server APIs.</p>`},
{id:'getting-started',group:'GETTING STARTED',title:'Quick Start',lead:'Create a keypoint, check a scope in a route, and start an HTTP listener.',html:`<h2>Initialize the framework</h2>${snippet("import { KeypointJS } from 'keypointjs';\n\nconst api = new KeypointJS({\n  enableAuditLog: false,\n  enableRateLimiter: false\n});")}<h2>Create an identity</h2>${snippet("await api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'demo-client',\n  secret: 'replace-with-a-local-secret',\n  scopes: ['orders:read'],\n  protocols: ['http']\n});")}<p>This local example selects HTTP. Keypoints default to <code>['https']</code>. The validator currently requires a keypoint ID but checks a secret only when a secret header is supplied.</p><h2>Protect a route</h2>${snippet("api.get('/orders', (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.hasScope('orders:read')) {\n    return ctx.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });\n  }\n  return ctx.json({ orders: [] });\n});\n\nawait api.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1');")}<h2>Send an authenticated request</h2>${snippet("curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/orders \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-ID: demo-client' \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-Secret: replace-with-a-local-secret'")}<p>Expected behavior: a JSON response with <code>orders: []</code> when validation and scope checks pass. See <a href="docs.html?page=authentication">Authentication</a> and <a href="docs.html?page=first-api">Build Your First API</a>.</p>`},
{id:'architecture',group:'CORE CONCEPTS',title:'Architecture',lead:'A composable request pipeline assembled by one KeyPointJS instance.',html:`<h2>Request layers</h2><ol><li>Pre-processing hook.</li><li>Protocol detection and request parsing.</li><li>Optional CORS middleware.</li><li>Keypoint validation when <code>requireKeypoint</code> is enabled.</li><li>Policy rules and optional named policy.</li><li>Plugin middleware.</li><li>Router handler.</li><li>Response headers and hooks.</li></ol><p>CORS is disabled by default. Keypoint validation is enabled by default. Policy rules, plugins, and routes run through the configured middleware chain.</p><h2>Core components</h2><ul><li><strong>ProtocolEngine:</strong> detects and parses request protocol data.</li><li><strong>KeypointValidator:</strong> loads and validates request identity.</li><li><strong>ScopeManager:</strong> resolves registered scope inheritance for contexts.</li><li><strong>PolicyEngine:</strong> evaluates global rules, then a selected named policy.</li><li><strong>MinimalRouter:</strong> matches method and pathname to a handler.</li><li><strong>PluginManager:</strong> runs plugin processing and hook callbacks.</li></ul><h2>Transport support boundaries</h2><p>The HTTP server uses Node's HTTP/HTTPS APIs. The repository contains WebSocket engine code and an optional <code>WebSocketGuard</code>. The gRPC server entry currently returns a placeholder object; HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 server support are not complete.</p>`},
{id:'lifecycle',group:'CORE CONCEPTS',title:'Request Lifecycle',lead:'What runs before a handler returns a response.',html:`<h2>Processing order</h2><p>For a regular HTTP request, the middleware executes in order: pre-validation hook, protocol engine, optional CORS, optional keypoint validation, policy evaluation, plugin processing, routing, then response processing.</p><h2>Context object</h2><p>Handlers receive a <code>KeypointContext</code>. It wraps the normalized request and provides response helpers such as <code>ctx.json(data, status)</code>, <code>ctx.text(data, status)</code>, <code>ctx.status(code)</code>, <code>ctx.hasScope(scope)</code>, and <code>ctx.getKeypointId()</code>.</p><h2>Success and failure</h2><p>A matched route can set <code>ctx.response</code> with context helpers or return a response-shaped object. Missing routes are answered with 404. Errors go to the configured error handler. The response processing stage adds default headers and invokes response hooks.</p>${callout('When requireKeypoint is false, a context may reach policies and routes without an attached keypoint. Route authorization must account for that configuration.',true)}`},
{id:'http-server',group:'CORE CONCEPTS',title:'HTTP Server',lead:'Use the promise-based server creation and listen methods provided by the framework.',html:`<h2>Start listening</h2>${snippet("const servers = await api.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1');\nconsole.log(servers.http.address());")}<p><code>listen(port, hostname, callback)</code> is asynchronous and resolves server handles. <code>createServer()</code> is also asynchronous: await it to obtain the Node HTTP server, then call its <code>listen()</code> method yourself.</p>${snippet("const server = await api.createServer();\nawait new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n  server.once('error', reject);\n  server.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', resolve);\n});")}<h2>HTTPS</h2><p>HTTPS server creation depends on passing TLS options through the framework's <code>https</code> option. Keep private key material out of source control and follow Node TLS guidance.</p><h2>Shutdown</h2><p><code>await api.shutdown()</code> runs plugin shutdown and WebSocket cleanup hooks. For a manually created server, also close the Node server handle so the process can exit cleanly.</p><h2>Transport limitations</h2><p>The HTTP/2, HTTP/3, and gRPC options do not create complete protocol servers in this implementation. See <a href="docs.html?page=faq">FAQ</a>.</p>`},
{id:'configuration',group:'CORE CONCEPTS',title:'Configuration',lead:'Set options when constructing the framework; several options only affect initialization.',html:`<h2>Common options</h2>${table(['Option','Default','Effect'],[['requireKeypoint','true','Adds keypoint validation middleware.'],['validateOrigin','true','Checks keypoint origin, if identity validation is active.'],['validateProtocol','true','Checks the keypoint protocol.'],['enableCORS','false','Adds CORS middleware.'],['corsOrigins',['*'],'CORS allowlist.'],['maxRequestSize','10mb','Request body limit passed to protocol engine.'],['trustedProxies','[]','Trusted sources for forwarded address handling.'],['enableWebSocket','true','Allows WebSocket guard attachment; the guard remains opt-in.'],['enableGrpc','false','Placeholder server functionality; not a working gRPC server.'],['keypointStorage','memory store','Storage instance; defaults to memory.'],['enableAuditLog','true','Registers built-in audit logger unless set false.'],['enableRateLimiter','true','Registers built-in rate limiter unless set false.']])}<h2>Constructor example</h2>${snippet("const api = new KeypointJS({\n  requireKeypoint: true,\n  validateOrigin: true,\n  enableCORS: false,\n  maxRequestSize: '10mb',\n  trustedProxies: ['10.0.0.2']\n});")}<h2>Configuration caveat</h2><p>Options are shallow-merged. <code>configure(options)</code> merges values after construction, but it does not reconstruct every subsystem. Use constructor options for settings consumed during initialization. See <a href="docs.html?page=configuration-reference">Configuration Reference</a>.</p>`},
{id:'api',group:'CORE CONCEPTS',title:'Core API',lead:'A practical overview of the framework entry point and request context.',html:`<h2>Framework methods</h2><p>Routes: <code>route</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>patch</code>, and <code>options</code>. Identity: <code>createKeypoint</code>, <code>getKeypoint</code>, <code>listKeypoints</code>, <code>revokeKeypoint</code>. Policy: <code>addPolicyRule</code>, <code>addPolicy</code>. Other methods include <code>use</code>, <code>registerPlugin</code>, <code>enableWebSocket</code>, <code>listen</code>, <code>createServer</code>, <code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>getStats</code>, <code>healthCheck</code>, and <code>shutdown</code>.</p><h2>Context methods</h2><p><code>json</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>html</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>setHeader</code>, <code>getHeader</code>, <code>getQuery</code>, <code>getAllQuery</code>, <code>setState</code>, <code>getState</code>, plus getters <code>body</code>, <code>protocol</code>, <code>ip</code>, <code>method</code>, and <code>path</code>.</p><h2>Public export paths</h2><p>Root package import: <code>keypointjs</code>. Additional exports include <code>keypointjs/keypoint</code>, <code>keypointjs/keypoint/context</code>, <code>keypointjs/keypoint/storage</code>, <code>keypointjs/keypoint/validator</code>, <code>keypointjs/keypoint/scopes</code>, <code>keypointjs/router</code>, <code>keypointjs/protocol</code>, <code>keypointjs/plugins/audit</code>, <code>keypointjs/plugins/ratelimit</code>, <code>keypointjs/plugins/websocket</code>, <code>keypointjs/plugins/transformer</code>, <code>keypointjs/policy</code>, <code>keypointjs/policy/rules</code>, <code>keypointjs/policy/decision</code>, and <code>keypointjs/html-parser</code>.</p>${callout('The declared keypointjs/plugins export currently points to a missing file. Import PluginManager and BuiltInHooks from the package root instead.',true)}<h2>More detail</h2><p>See <a href="docs.html?page=api-reference">API Reference</a> for signatures and API-specific notes.</p>`},
{id:'authentication',group:'SECURITY',title:'Authentication',lead:'Keypoint identity validation is the framework request authentication mechanism.',html:`<h2>Create a keypoint</h2>${snippet("await api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'service-client',\n  secret: 'generated-secret-value',\n  scopes: ['orders:read'],\n  protocols: ['https'],\n  expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 86_400_000)\n});")}<h2>Validation behavior</h2><ol><li>Extract ID from <code>X-Keypoint-ID</code> or parsed query parameter <code>keypointId</code>.</li><li>Load the keypoint from configured storage.</li><li>Reject missing/unknown identity or expired records.</li><li>If <code>X-Keypoint-Secret</code> is sent, compare it with the stored secret.</li><li>Attach keypoint to context; check origin and protocol if configured.</li></ol>${callout('The secret header is currently optional. Secret comparison is plain string equality. Do not describe this as a complete credential system.',true)}<h2>Origin and protocol</h2><p>Keypoints default to protocol <code>https</code>. Empty <code>allowedOrigins</code> accepts any origin. If the request has neither Origin nor Referer, context origin validation passes. Configure explicit values and test the deployment flow.</p><h2>Request example</h2>${snippet("curl https://api.example.test/orders \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-ID: service-client' \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-Secret: generated-secret-value'")}<p>See <a href="docs.html?page=security">security recommendations</a>.</p>`},
{id:'authorization',group:'SECURITY',title:'Authorization',lead:'Combine scopes, policies, and route-level decisions for resource access.',html:`<h2>Check permissions in a route</h2>${snippet("api.get('/reports', (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.hasScope('reports:read')) {\n    return ctx.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });\n  }\n  return ctx.json({ reports: [] });\n});")}<h2>Use context scope checks</h2><p><code>ctx.hasScope</code>, <code>ctx.hasAnyScope</code>, and <code>ctx.hasAllScopes</code> resolve registered inheritance through the instance ScopeManager. Direct <code>Keypoint.hasScope</code> checks literal scope names and wildcard only; prefer request context for authorization.</p><h2>Policy enforcement</h2><p>Global policy rules run before a named policy. Rules are evaluated in registration order and stop on first denial. A selected policy comes from <code>keypoint.metadata.policy</code>. If no matching named policy is configured, global rules passing results in allow. Design policies with that behavior in mind.</p><h2>Denied behavior</h2><p>Policy denial becomes a <code>PolicyError</code>, typically an HTTP 403 response via the configured error handler. A 404 indicates route absence, not an authorization decision.</p>`},
{id:'scopes',group:'SECURITY',title:'Scope System',lead:'Scope definitions describe permission names; inheritance must be explicitly registered.',html:`<h2>Define scopes</h2>${snippet("api.defineScope('reports:read', 'Read reports');\napi.defineScope('reports:write', 'Modify reports');\napi.scopeManager.addInheritance('reports:write', ['reports:read']);")}<p>Scope definitions can include metadata. The manager includes defaults such as <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>admin</code>, API scopes, and resource examples.</p><h2>Inheritance direction</h2><p>A granted parent includes the scopes registered as its children, recursively. For example, <code>admin → write</code> and <code>write → read</code> means a keypoint with <code>admin</code> satisfies checks for admin, write, and read.</p>${snippet("api.scopeManager.addInheritance('admin', ['write']);\napi.scopeManager.addInheritance('write', ['read']);\n// Context granted admin: hasScope('read') is true")}<h2>Any and all checks</h2><p><code>ctx.hasAnyScope([...])</code> passes if one required scope is available; <code>ctx.hasAllScopes([...])</code> requires every scope. Circular edges are cycle-safe.</p><h2>Unknown scopes</h2><p><code>validateScope</code> checks definitions, but keypoint creation does not automatically validate assigned scopes. Validate identity provisioning in your application. Only explicitly registered inheritance edges grant descendants.</p>`},
{id:'wildcard',group:'SECURITY',title:'Wildcard Permissions',lead:'The literal wildcard scope grants broad access and should be assigned deliberately.',html:`<h2>Wildcard behavior</h2><p>A keypoint containing <code>*</code> passes any scope check in the ScopeManager and the literal Keypoint helper. This includes scope names that are not registered.</p>${snippet("await api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'unrestricted-service',\n  scopes: ['*'],\n  protocols: ['https']\n});")}<h2>Security considerations</h2><ul><li>Avoid wildcard for ordinary end-user and service identities.</li><li>Prefer resource-specific scopes and explicit inheritance.</li><li>Audit provisioning flows so a wildcard cannot be added through untrusted input.</li><li>When using <code>ctx.hasAllScopes</code>, remember wildcard satisfies each member.</li></ul>${callout('Do not confuse an arbitrary string containing an asterisk with wildcard access. Pattern matching helpers are a separate API and are not used by hasScope.',true)}`},
{id:'policy',group:'SECURITY',title:'Policy Engine',lead:'Global rules run in order, then the engine selects an optional named policy.',html:`<h2>Register rules</h2>${snippet("import { BuiltInRules } from 'keypointjs';\n\napi.addPolicyRule(BuiltInRules.methodRule(['GET', 'POST']));\napi.addPolicyRule(BuiltInRules.scopeRule('orders:read'));\napi.addPolicyRule(BuiltInRules.originRule(['https://app.example']));")}<h2>Implemented rule factories</h2><p><code>methodRule</code>, <code>originRule</code>, <code>ipRule</code>, <code>timeWindowRule</code>, <code>rateLimitRule</code>, <code>scopeRule</code>, and <code>protocolRule</code>. Rules are appended in order. Rule priority is stored on PolicyRule but is not used to sort.</p><h2>Named policy</h2>${snippet("api.addPolicy('orders-read', api.policyEngine.allow({\n  scope: 'orders:read',\n  method: 'GET',\n  protocol: 'https'\n}));\n\nawait api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'reader', scopes: ['orders:read'],\n  protocols: ['https'], metadata: { policy: 'orders-read' }\n});")}<h2>Important limits</h2><p>The engine returns plain decision objects, despite exporting an <code>AccessDecision</code> class. The IP rule's CIDR matching is simplified, and empty origin rules permit any origin. The rate rule is in-process. Do not treat these as complete security boundary controls without further validation.</p>`},
{id:'cors',group:'SECURITY',title:'CORS',lead:'CORS is opt-in and controls browser cross-origin response headers, not API authorization.',html:`<h2>Enable CORS</h2>${snippet("const api = new KeypointJS({\n  enableCORS: true,\n  corsOrigins: ['https://app.example']\n});")}<p>The default <code>enableCORS</code> is false. When enabled, the middleware checks the request Origin against <code>corsOrigins</code>. The default list is <code>['*']</code>; replace it with an explicit list where appropriate.</p><h2>Preflight</h2><p>OPTIONS requests are handled by CORS middleware when it is enabled; the router also responds 204 for OPTIONS on a registered path with an Allow header. CORS headers depend on the configured origin match.</p><h2>Security note</h2>${callout('CORS is enforced by browsers and is not a substitute for keypoint validation, scopes, CSRF controls, or server-side authorization.')}`},
{id:'http-errors',group:'SECURITY',title:'404 and OPTIONS Handling',lead:'Missing paths and valid preflight requests use distinct router outcomes.',html:`<h2>Missing route</h2><p>When no method/path handler matches, the router returns JSON with status 404 and <code>{error: 'Not Found', code: 404}</code>. This is different from policy denial, which normally returns 403.</p><h2>OPTIONS on a known path</h2><p>If OPTIONS has no explicit handler but other methods are registered for that pathname, the router returns 204 with an <code>Allow</code> header containing those methods plus OPTIONS.</p><h2>Unknown path</h2><p>When no route exists for the requested path, the router returns 404, including for OPTIONS unless CORS middleware handles that request earlier.</p><h2>Example</h2>${snippet("api.get('/items', (ctx) => ctx.json([]));\n\n// OPTIONS /items -> 204 and Allow header\n// GET /missing -> 404 JSON response")}<p>Preflight headers are only added by the CORS middleware when <code>enableCORS</code> is true and the origin is allowed.</p>`},
{id:'security',group:'SECURITY',title:'Security Best Practices',lead:'Distinguish implementation controls from the additional protections your application needs.',html:`<h2>Controls present in the framework</h2><ul><li>Keypoint lookup and expiration checks.</li><li>Optional validation of a supplied secret header.</li><li>Protocol and origin checks when configured.</li><li>Scope and policy extension points.</li><li>Body size parsing limits and trusted-proxy-aware address extraction.</li></ul><h2>Application responsibilities</h2><ul><li>Use TLS and generate high-entropy credentials.</li><li>Protect credential storage; built-in file storage is plain JSON.</li><li>Set least-privilege scopes and validate provisioning inputs.</li><li>Configure explicit origin policy as needed.</li><li>Restrict trusted proxies to known infrastructure.</li><li>Review logs for sensitive data and establish retention controls.</li><li>Test rate limiting and error behavior for your deployment.</li></ul><h2>Known limitations</h2><p>Secret header is not mandatory; equality comparison is plain string. <code>allowedIps</code> on Keypoint is not enforced by request validation. IP policy CIDR matching is simplified. Built-in rate limiting is in-process and has implementation limits. There is no claim of external security audit or production readiness.</p>`},
{id:'middleware',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'Middleware',lead:'Add request processing functions with the public use(middleware) method.',html:`<h2>Middleware signature</h2><p>Middleware receives <code>(ctx, next)</code>. Call and await <code>next()</code> to continue the chain. Add custom middleware with <code>api.use(fn)</code>.</p>${snippet("api.use(async (ctx, next) => {\n  ctx.setState('requestStartedAt', Date.now());\n  return next();\n});")}<h2>Order matters</h2><p>Custom middleware appended using <code>use</code> joins the configured chain. Framework layers are initialized during construction. Use hooks for specific framework lifecycle points and test ordering where policy depends on context state.</p><h2>Context state</h2><p>Use <code>ctx.setState(key, value)</code> and <code>ctx.getState(key)</code> to pass request-local values. Plugin-specific data has separate helpers <code>setPluginData</code> and <code>getPluginData</code>.</p><h2>Errors</h2><p>Throwing rejects the middleware chain and is passed to the framework error handler. Do not swallow errors unless the middleware intentionally converts them into a response.</p>`},
{id:'hooks',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'Hooks',lead:'Hooks observe named lifecycle moments and can run asynchronous callbacks.',html:`<h2>Register a hook</h2>${snippet("api.pluginManager.addHook('before_route_execution', async (ctx) => {\n  // Observe request state before route handler runs.\n});")}<p>Hook names are available from the root export <code>BuiltInHooks</code>, including <code>BEFORE_KEYPOINT_VALIDATION</code>, <code>AFTER_KEYPOINT_VALIDATION</code>, <code>BEFORE_POLICY_CHECK</code>, <code>AFTER_POLICY_CHECK</code>, <code>BEFORE_ROUTE_EXECUTION</code>, <code>AFTER_ROUTE_EXECUTION</code>, <code>BEFORE_RESPONSE</code>, <code>AFTER_RESPONSE</code>, <code>ON_ERROR</code>, <code>INITIALIZE</code>, and <code>SHUTDOWN</code>.</p><h2>Error handling behavior</h2><p>The manager awaits hooks sequentially. Errors thrown from hooks are logged and swallowed; they do not fail the request chain. Do not use hooks as a mandatory transaction or enforcement mechanism unless you separately verify their behavior.</p><h2>Plugins can declare hooks</h2><p>A plugin may expose a <code>hooks</code> object whose entries map hook names to functions. Registration attaches those functions to the manager.</p>`},
{id:'plugins',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'Plugin System',lead:'Register middleware-capable objects and preserve the configured plugin options.',html:`<h2>Register a plugin</h2>${snippet("const audit = new AuditLogger({ logToConsole: false });\napi.registerPlugin(audit, { logToFile: false });")}<p>Plugins are keyed by constructor name. Registration merges explicit registration options over existing plugin options and rejects duplicate names. Plugins with a <code>process(ctx, next)</code> method join request processing in registration order.</p><h2>Built-in plugins</h2><ul><li><code>AuditLogger</code> is registered by default unless <code>enableAuditLog: false</code>.</li><li><code>RateLimiter</code> is registered by default unless <code>enableRateLimiter: false</code>; see its documented limitations.</li><li><code>WebSocketGuard</code> is opt-in through <code>api.enableWebSocket(options)</code>.</li></ul><h2>Lifecycle and manager</h2><p><code>api.pluginManager</code> supports registration, enable/disable, lookup, configuration, hooks, initialization, shutdown, and stats. On shutdown, plugins with a shutdown method are awaited; cleanup behavior depends on each plugin.</p><h2>Custom plugin outline</h2>${snippet("class RequestMarker {\n  constructor(options = {}) { this.options = options; }\n  async process(ctx, next) {\n    ctx.setPluginData('RequestMarker', { active: true });\n    return next(ctx);\n  }\n}\napi.registerPlugin(new RequestMarker({ mode: 'safe' }));")}<p>Use the root plugin-manager export; the current <code>keypointjs/plugins</code> package subpath points to a missing source file.</p>`},
{id:'websocket',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'WebSocket Lifecycle',lead:'WebSocketGuard is opt-in and validates a keypoint ID during the handshake by default.',html:`<h2>Enable the guard</h2>${snippet("const guard = api.enableWebSocket({\n  path: '/ws',\n  requireKeypoint: true,\n  maxConnections: 100,\n  pingInterval: 30000\n});")}<p>The guard is attached when KeyPointJS creates its HTTP server. The default handshake checks the configured keypoint header and validates the stored keypoint. The request uses the same KeypointValidator behavior.</p><h2>Connection lifecycle</h2><p>Register <code>onConnection</code> and <code>onDisconnection</code> callbacks. A ping interval checks last activity, sends pings, and terminates idle connections. Configure <code>maxConnections</code>; pending handshakes count toward the limit.</p><h2>Messages</h2>${snippet("guard.onMessage('chat', async (message, connection) => {\n  if (!connection.scopes?.includes('chat:write')) {\n    return { type: 'error', error: 'Forbidden' };\n  }\n  return { type: 'ack' };\n});")}<p>Perform authorization for each sensitive message. The guard's handshake path does not automatically provide complete per-message authorization or keypoint origin/protocol checks.</p><h2>Cleanup</h2><p><code>guard.cleanup()</code> clears the recurring timer, closes active connections and its WebSocket server, and resets pending counts.</p>`},
{id:'html-parser',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'HTML Parser',lead:'An optional parse5-based HTML parser with a small DOM-like facade.',html:`<h2>Import and parse</h2>${snippet("import { HTMLParser } from 'keypointjs/html-parser';\n\nconst document = HTMLParser.parse('<main><h1>Hi</h1></main>');\nconsole.log(document.querySelector('h1')?.textContent); // Hi")}<p>Use <code>parseFragment</code> for fragments that may have multiple roots. Parser limits include maximum input bytes, DOM node count, and nesting depth. Defaults and complete selector support are documented in the <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/blob/main/docs/html-parser.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">package HTML parser guide</a>.</p><h2>Facade coverage</h2><p>Document and fragment querying, basic tree relationships, element attributes, text content, inner/outer HTML, and serialization are supported. CSS selector support is a subset, not full browser compatibility.</p><h2>Isolation and safety</h2><ul><li>The module is imported from its own package subpath.</li><li>It does not fetch URLs or external assets.</li><li>It does not execute scripts.</li><li>It is not a complete browser DOM.</li></ul><p>WebScraper is not currently implemented.</p>`},
{id:'optional-dependencies',group:'ADVANCED FEATURES',title:'Optional Dependencies',lead:'The main API entry point can be used without loading optional HTML parser dependencies.',html:`<h2>Core package</h2><p>The core framework imports through <code>keypointjs</code>. Parser packages are declared as optional dependencies and are loaded by the HTML parser module, not by the main entry point.</p>${snippet('npm install keypointjs --omit=optional')}<p>Core APIs do not use the parser packages. If you later import <code>keypointjs/html-parser</code>, it will report a missing parser dependency and its package installation command.</p><h2>Install parser support</h2>${snippet('npm install parse5@7.3.0 css-select@5.2.2 css-what@6.2.2')}<h2>Keep application dependencies deliberate</h2><p>Optional dependency installation behavior depends on the package manager and configuration. Confirm the installed dependency tree for your deployment environment.</p>`},
{id:'first-api',group:'GUIDES',title:'Build Your First API',lead:'A small runnable ES module using the public npm import and framework HTTP listener.',html:`<h2>Set up the project</h2>${snippet('npm init -y\nnpm pkg set type=module\nnpm install keypointjs')}<h2>Create server.js</h2>${snippet("import { KeypointJS } from 'keypointjs';\n\nconst api = new KeypointJS({\n  enableAuditLog: false,\n  enableRateLimiter: false\n});\n\nawait api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'local-app',\n  secret: 'local-development-secret',\n  scopes: ['notes:read'],\n  protocols: ['http']\n});\n\napi.get('/notes', (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.hasScope('notes:read')) {\n    return ctx.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });\n  }\n  return ctx.json({ notes: [] });\n});\n\nawait api.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1');")}<h2>Run and call</h2>${snippet("node server.js\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:3000/notes \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-ID: local-app' \\\n  -H 'X-Keypoint-Secret: local-development-secret'")}<p>Expected output: <code>{"notes":[]}</code>. For a real deployment, use TLS, generated secrets, persisted storage, and an explicit review of the security limits. See <a href="docs.html?page=production">Deployment Considerations</a>.</p>`},
{id:'protect-endpoints',group:'GUIDES',title:'Protecting API Endpoints',lead:'Pair identity validation with a least-privilege permission check at each sensitive operation.',html:`<h2>Issue separate identities</h2>${snippet("await api.createKeypoint({ keyId: 'reader', scopes: ['orders:read'], protocols: ['https'] });\nawait api.createKeypoint({ keyId: 'writer', scopes: ['orders:read', 'orders:write'], protocols: ['https'] });")}<h2>Check each route</h2>${snippet("api.get('/orders', (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.hasScope('orders:read')) return ctx.status(403);\n  return ctx.json({ orders: [] });\n});\n\napi.post('/orders', (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.hasScope('orders:write')) return ctx.status(403);\n  return ctx.json({ created: true }, 201);\n});")}<h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Assuming a valid keypoint automatically authorizes every action.</li><li>Using wildcard scopes for convenience.</li><li>Relying only on frontend visibility instead of server-side checks.</li><li>Assuming <code>allowedIps</code> is enforced automatically.</li></ul>`},
{id:'scope-inheritance',group:'GUIDES',title:'Scope Inheritance',lead:'Grant a broad parent scope that explicitly inherits narrower permissions.',html:`<h2>Register the tree</h2>${snippet("api.defineScope('team:admin', 'Manage the team');\napi.defineScope('team:write', 'Edit team resources');\napi.defineScope('team:read', 'Read team resources');\napi.scopeManager.addInheritance('team:admin', ['team:write']);\napi.scopeManager.addInheritance('team:write', ['team:read']);")}<h2>Assign only the parent</h2>${snippet("await api.createKeypoint({\n  keyId: 'team-operator',\n  scopes: ['team:admin'],\n  protocols: ['https']\n});")}<p>A request context for this identity passes <code>ctx.hasScope('team:read')</code>, because the registered hierarchy is traversed from granted parent to descendant. Cycles are bounded and duplicate inherited scopes do not change decisions.</p><h2>Keep the hierarchy intentional</h2><p>Inheritance grants all descendants. Review the full set using <code>scopeManager.getInheritedScopes('team:admin')</code> and avoid accidentally linking privileged parents to broad scopes.</p>`},
{id:'wildcard-guide',group:'GUIDES',title:'Wildcard Access',lead:'Treat the wildcard scope as an unrestricted grant.',html:`<h2>What * means</h2><p>A keypoint assigned the literal <code>*</code> is treated as having every requested scope, including unknown scope strings. This behavior is direct and intentionally broad.</p><h2>Safer alternatives</h2><ul><li>Create resource scopes such as <code>billing:read</code> and <code>billing:write</code>.</li><li>Use explicit inheritance for roles.</li><li>Provision an identity per service or operational purpose.</li><li>Review scope assignments and revoke identities that no longer need access.</li></ul>${callout('Do not use wildcard as a generic fallback for missing or invalid scope configuration.',true)}`},
{id:'error-handling',group:'GUIDES',title:'Error Handling',lead:'Framework request failures are converted by the configured errorHandler.',html:`<h2>Default response format</h2><p>The default error handler returns a response with <code>status</code>, JSON content type, and an error body containing message/code/timestamp/requestId. Error detail visibility depends on error class, status, and strictMode.</p><h2>Customize error handling</h2>${snippet("const api = new KeypointJS({\n  errorHandler(error, ctx) {\n    const status = error.code || 500;\n    return {\n      status,\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: { error: status >= 500 ? 'Internal Server Error' : error.message }\n    };\n  }\n});")}<p>This matches the framework's response object contract. Avoid returning stack traces, credentials, or sensitive metadata to clients.</p><h2>Common statuses</h2>${table(['Status','Typical case'],[['401','Missing, unknown, expired keypoint, or supplied secret mismatch.'],['403','Origin/protocol rejection or policy denial.'],['404','No route matched the method and path.'],['500','Unexpected handler or internal error.']])}<p>Exact details depend on custom error handling and current configuration.</p>`},
{id:'production',group:'GUIDES',title:'Deployment Considerations',lead:'A checklist for evaluating the framework in your own environment; not a production-readiness claim.',html:`<h2>Before exposing an API</h2><ul><li>Use HTTPS and ensure keypoint protocol restrictions match the external request.</li><li>Generate secrets securely and understand the current optional-secret behavior.</li><li>Protect storage files and backups; built-in JSON storage is not encrypted.</li><li>Review CORS and origin allowlists.</li><li>Configure proxy trust for actual known proxies only.</li><li>Test policy decisions, route errors, body limits, and shutdown behavior.</li><li>Establish centralized, distributed rate limiting where your threat model requires it.</li><li>Audit scopes, named policies, plugin order, and logging data.</li></ul><h2>Known gaps to account for</h2><p>Per-keypoint allowed IP enforcement is absent. The policy CIDR matcher is simplified. Built-in limiter behavior is in-memory. The secret comparison is direct and the secret is optional. gRPC/HTTP2/HTTP3 are incomplete. These limitations need remediation or compensating controls before relying on the related feature.</p><h2>Operational review</h2><p>Run application-specific security testing and establish monitoring, alerting, credential rotation, backup, and incident response procedures. KeyPointJS does not make those decisions for your application.</p>`},
{id:'api-reference',group:'REFERENCE',title:'API Reference',lead:'A high-level index of package exports. Detailed signatures should be checked against the version you install.',html:`<h2>Root package exports</h2><p><code>KeypointJS</code>, <code>Context</code>, <code>ProtocolEngine</code>, <code>ProtocolError</code>, <code>Keypoint</code>, <code>KeypointContext</code>, <code>KeypointValidator</code>, <code>KeypointError</code>, <code>MemoryKeypointStorage</code>, <code>ScopeManager</code>, <code>PolicyEngine</code>, <code>BuiltInRules</code>, <code>MinimalRouter</code>, <code>PluginManager</code>, <code>BuiltInHooks</code>, <code>RateLimiter</code>, <code>AuditLogger</code>, <code>WebSocketGuard</code>, <code>AccessDecision</code>, <code>PolicyError</code>, <code>ValidationError</code>, and <code>ProtocolExamples</code>.</p><h2>Subpath exports</h2>${table(['Import path','Main export'],[['keypointjs/keypoint','Keypoint'],['keypointjs/keypoint/context','KeypointContext'],['keypointjs/keypoint/storage','KeypointStorage, MemoryKeypointStorage, FileKeypointStorage'],['keypointjs/keypoint/validator','KeypointValidator'],['keypointjs/keypoint/scopes','ScopeManager'],['keypointjs/router','MinimalRouter'],['keypointjs/protocol','ProtocolAdapter'],['keypointjs/plugins/audit','AuditLogger'],['keypointjs/plugins/ratelimit','RateLimiter'],['keypointjs/plugins/websocket','WebSocketGuard'],['keypointjs/plugins/transformer','TransformerPlugin'],['keypointjs/policy','PolicyEngine'],['keypointjs/policy/rules','PolicyRule, BuiltInRules'],['keypointjs/policy/decision','AccessDecision'],['keypointjs/html-parser','HTMLParser']])}<h2>Notable return behavior</h2><p><code>createKeypoint</code>, storage operations, <code>listen</code>, and <code>createServer</code> are asynchronous. Route helpers and <code>use</code>/<code>addPolicyRule</code> return the framework for chaining; <code>defineScope</code> currently does not return it.</p>${callout('The package defines keypointjs/plugins pointing at a source file that is absent in the repository. Import PluginManager and BuiltInHooks from the root export.',true)}<h2>Full reference</h2><p>Use the package source and <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">repository</a> when a signature is not listed here. Internal helpers are not promised as stable API.</p>`},
{id:'configuration-reference',group:'REFERENCE',title:'Configuration Reference',lead:'Options checked against KeypointJS constructor and subsystem setup.',html:`<h2>Identity and transport</h2>${table(['Option','Default','Notes'],[['requireKeypoint','true','Controls insertion of validation middleware.'],['validateOrigin','true','Origin check after keypoint validation.'],['validateProtocol','true','Protocol check after keypoint validation.'],['trustedProxies','[]','Forwarded IP/proto trust list.'],['maxRequestSize','10mb','Passed to ProtocolEngine maxBodySize.'],['protocolEngines','{}','Custom protocol engine registrations.'],['enableWebSocket','true','Permits WebSocket guard setup.'],['enableGrpc','false','gRPC server implementation is placeholder.'],['enableHttp2','true','Does not currently create an HTTP/2 server.'],['enableHttp3','false','No HTTP/3 server implementation.']])}<h2>CORS and responses</h2>${table(['Option','Default','Notes'],[['enableCORS','false','Adds CORS middleware.'],['corsOrigins',['*'],'Wildcard by default; explicitly configure.'],['defaultResponseHeaders','X-Powered-By, nosniff','Merged with response headers.'],['errorHandler','defaultErrorHandler','Custom response handler for errors.'],['strictMode','true','Affects detail exposure for selected error classes.']])}<h2>Plugins and storage</h2><p>Use <code>keypointStorage</code> to inject a storage instance. Built-in <code>AuditLogger</code> and <code>RateLimiter</code> are enabled unless their flags are false. Audit options include <code>auditToConsole</code>, <code>auditToFile</code>, and <code>auditFilePath</code>. Rate options are forwarded but do not fully match effective enforcement—see <a href="docs.html?page=policy">Policy Engine</a> and <a href="docs.html?page=security">Security</a>.</p><h2>Post-construction changes</h2><p><code>configure(options)</code> shallow merges options but does not reinitialize all components. Configure initialization-sensitive values in constructor.</p>`},
{id:'error-reference',group:'REFERENCE',title:'Error Reference',lead:'Common error classes and default HTTP mappings.',html:`<h2>Exported errors</h2>${table(['Class','Default code','Use'],[['KeypointError','401','Keypoint validation issues; code can be specified.'],['ProtocolError','400','Invalid/unsupported request protocol processing.'],['PolicyError','403','Policy denial; can include a decision.'],['ValidationError','400','General validation error with an errors array.'],['Error','500','Unexpected errors unless custom code/status is set.']])}<h2>Default handler</h2><p>The default handler derives response status from <code>error.code</code> or uses 500. It returns JSON and may omit sensitive error details in strict mode. For errors with status below 500, it exposes the message under its current logic.</p><h2>Route not found</h2><p>404 is generated as a normal router response, not an exception. OPTIONS on a known path can return 204 with Allow. See <a href="docs.html?page=http-errors">404 and OPTIONS Handling</a>.</p><h2>Custom handler caveats</h2><p>Return a response object containing <code>status</code>, optional <code>headers</code>, and <code>body</code>. Ensure errors do not leak secrets, stack traces, or internal configuration.</p>`},
{id:'changelog',group:'REFERENCE',title:'Changelog',lead:'This website does not duplicate unpublished release notes or invent change summaries.',html:`<h2>Current package reference</h2><p>Repository package metadata lists version <strong>1.3.0</strong>. Published registry versions may differ; npm is the canonical source for the package currently installable by consumers.</p><div class="callout">This repository has no CHANGELOG.md at the time this page was prepared. Review the package repository history and published release page for actual changes.</div><p><a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub Releases ↗</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/commits/main" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Commit history ↗</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/keypointjs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">npm package ↗</a></p>`},
{id:'migration',group:'REFERENCE',title:'Migration Guide',lead:'No official migration path from another framework is currently maintained.',status:'PLANNED',html:`<h2>Availability</h2><p>A migration guide for moving from Express, Fastify, or other identity systems has not been verified as part of the project. We are not publishing a fabricated set of migration steps.</p><h2>General adoption approach</h2><p>For evaluation, start with a separate route or service, map existing identities to Keypoint records, test access decisions, and check runtime/error behavior. Do not change authentication providers or production traffic until your migration plan accounts for sessions, secret lifecycle, revocation, logs, and rollback.</p><p>Open a repository discussion or issue to propose a concrete migration target and version.</p>`},
{id:'faq',group:'REFERENCE',title:'FAQ',lead:'Short answers about what the current implementation provides.',html:`<h2>Does KeyPointJS require Express?</h2><p>No. It uses Node.js HTTP APIs and provides its own small routing system.</p><h2>Is the secret header mandatory?</h2><p>No. Keypoint ID is required by the validator, but secret equality is checked only when a secret header is provided.</p><h2>Does allowedIps enforce caller addresses?</h2><p>No. The property is stored but not automatically enforced by the validator. A separate policy rule exists, with simplified CIDR behavior.</p><h2>Is gRPC or HTTP/2 ready?</h2><p>The code contains protocol detection/engine components, but the server integrations are incomplete or placeholder. Do not rely on them as supported server transports.</p><h2>Is the HTML parser a browser DOM?</h2><p>No. It offers a limited facade and CSS selector subset. It does not execute JavaScript or load external resources.</p><h2>Is WebScraper available?</h2><p>No. The parser does not fetch URLs; WebScraper has not been implemented.</p>`},
{id:'contributing',group:'REFERENCE',title:'Contributing',lead:'Help improve the implementation and keep documentation aligned with behavior.',html:`<h2>Project links</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub repository</a> · <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Report an issue</a> · <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Contributing guide</a> · <a href="https://github.com/anasbex-dev/keypointjs/blob/main/LICENSE" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apache-2.0 license</a></p><h2>Helpful reports</h2><ul><li>Include Node.js and package versions.</li><li>Provide a minimal reproduction and expected/actual result.</li><li>For security concerns, follow the repository's private reporting instructions where available.</li><li>Keep documentation claims tied to tested and implemented behavior.</li></ul><h2>Run project tests</h2>${snippet('npm run test:all')}<p>Do not interpret test success as an independent security audit.</p>`}
];

const docsNav = document.querySelector('#docsNav');
const article = document.querySelector('#docsArticle');
const toc = document.querySelector('#pageToc');
const searchInput = document.querySelector('#docsSearch');
const searchResults = document.querySelector('#searchResults');
const menuButton = document.querySelector('#docsMenuToggle');
const sidebar = document.querySelector('#docsSidebar');
const backdrop = document.querySelector('#sidebarBackdrop');
const toast = document.querySelector('#toast');
const available = new Map(pages.map((page) => [page.id, page]));
let toastTimer;

function currentPageId() {
  const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
  return params.get('page') || 'getting-started';
}

function setMenu(open) {
  sidebar.classList.toggle('open', open);
  backdrop.classList.toggle('visible', open);
  menuButton.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
  menuButton.setAttribute('aria-label', open ? 'Close documentation navigation' : 'Open documentation navigation');
}

function renderNavigation(current) {
  docsNav.innerHTML = groups.map((group) => `<div class="nav-group"><div class="nav-group-title">${group.title}</div>${group.pages.map(([id, title]) => `<a class="doc-nav-link${id === current ? ' active' : ''}" href="docs.html?page=${id}" data-page="${id}">${title}${id === 'migration' ? '<span class="planned">PLANNED</span>' : ''}</a>`).join('')}</div>`).join('');
  docsNav.querySelectorAll('[data-page]').forEach((link) => link.addEventListener('click', () => setMenu(false)));
}

function renderSearch() {
  const query = searchInput.value.trim().toLowerCase();
  if (!query) { searchResults.hidden = true; return; }
  const matches = pages.filter((page) => `${page.title} ${page.group} ${page.lead} ${page.html.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, ' ')}`.toLowerCase().includes(query)).slice(0, 8);
  searchResults.innerHTML = matches.length ? matches.map((page) => `<a href="docs.html?page=${page.id}"><b>${page.title}</b><small>${page.group}</small></a>`).join('') : '<div class="search-empty">No matching topics</div>';
  searchResults.hidden = false;
}

function renderToc() {
  const headings = [...article.querySelectorAll('h2, h3')];
  headings.forEach((heading, index) => {
    heading.id = `section-${index + 1}`;
  });
  toc.innerHTML = headings.map((heading) => `<a class="${heading.tagName === 'H3' ? 'sub' : ''}" href="#${heading.id}">${heading.textContent}</a>`).join('');
  toc.querySelectorAll('a').forEach((link) => link.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    document.getElementById(link.hash.slice(1))?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
    history.replaceState(null, '', `${location.pathname}${location.search}${link.hash}`);
  }));
  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => entries.forEach((entry) => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        toc.querySelectorAll('a').forEach((link) => link.classList.toggle('active', link.hash === `#${entry.target.id}`));
      }
    }), { rootMargin: '-90px 0px -72% 0px' });
    headings.forEach((heading) => observer.observe(heading));
  }
}

function renderPage() {
  let page = available.get(currentPageId());
  if (!page) page = available.get('getting-started');
  const sequence = groups.flatMap((item) => item.pages);
  const index = sequence.findIndex(([id]) => id === page.id);
  const prev = index > 0 ? sequence[index - 1] : null;
  const next = index < sequence.length - 1 ? sequence[index + 1] : null;
  const allSections = page.group.split(' ');
  const status = page.status ? `<div class="doc-status planned">◷ &nbsp;${page.status} · not implemented</div>` : '';
  article.innerHTML = `<p class="doc-kicker">${allSections.join(' / ')}</p><h1>${page.title}</h1><p class="doc-lead">${page.lead}</p>${status}${page.html}`;
  document.title = `${page.title} — KeyPointJS Docs`;
  document.querySelector('#breadcrumbs').innerHTML = `<a href="index.html">KeyPointJS</a><span>/</span><a href="docs.html?page=${page.id}">Documentation</a><span>/</span><span>${page.title}</span>`;
  document.querySelector('#prevNext').innerHTML = `${prev ? `<a href="docs.html?page=${prev[0]}"><small>← PREVIOUS</small><b>${prev[1]}</b></a>` : '<span></span>'}${next ? `<a href="docs.html?page=${next[0]}"><small>NEXT →</small><b>${next[1]}</b></a>` : '<span></span>'}`;
  article.querySelectorAll('.code-copy').forEach((button) => button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.nextElementSibling.textContent);
      button.textContent = 'Copied';
      clearTimeout(toastTimer);
      toast.textContent = 'Code copied';
      toast.classList.add('show');
      toastTimer = setTimeout(() => toast.classList.remove('show'), 1500);
      setTimeout(() => { button.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1300);
    } catch { button.textContent = 'Select code to copy'; setTimeout(() => { button.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1600); }
  }));
  renderNavigation(page.id);
  renderToc();
  searchResults.hidden = true;
  searchInput.value = '';
  window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'instant' });
}

searchInput.addEventListener('input', renderSearch);
document.addEventListener('click', (event) => { if (!event.target.closest('.sidebar-top')) searchResults.hidden = true; });
searchInput.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.key === 'Escape') { searchResults.hidden = true; searchInput.blur(); }
  if (event.key === 'Enter') searchResults.querySelector('a')?.click();
});
document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.key === '/' && !['INPUT','TEXTAREA'].includes(document.activeElement.tagName)) { event.preventDefault(); searchInput.focus(); }
});
menuButton.addEventListener('click', () => setMenu(!sidebar.classList.contains('open')));
backdrop.addEventListener('click', () => setMenu(false));
window.addEventListener('popstate', renderPage);
renderPage();
